---
import SaidDocsLayout from "../../../layouts/SaidDocsLayout.astro";
import SaidShot from "../../../components/SaidShot.astro";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "HowTo",
  name: "Connect your AI agent to your Said and Done. desk",
  description:
    "Connect an MCP agent over HTTP or stdio and let it file its own work under its own name, on the Mars or Bust sample project.",
  step: [
    { "@type": "HowToStep", name: "Pick a door — HTTP or stdio" },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", name: "Connect Claude Code in one line" },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", name: "Ask for the board" },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", name: "Have it file a card" },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", name: "Check its work on the board" },
  ],
};
---

<SaidDocsLayout
  title="Let your agent in — Said and Done. guides"
  description="Connect an MCP agent to your Said and Done. desk — two doors, one line each — and watch it file real cards under its own name."
  section="guides"
  kicker="Guide 03"
  heading="Let your agent in"
  dek="Two doors, one line, a named teammate."
  slug="agents-at-the-desk"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p>
    Said and Done. speaks MCP — the protocol AI agents use to work with tools.
    Connected, an agent can list your boards, search your notes, file cards,
    move them, resolve them, and speak in card threads. Every action is signed
    with the agent's own name, exactly like yours. Everything below was done for
    real against the <em>Mars or Bust</em> sample project — the one the first-boot
    wizard offers you — so you can follow along click for click.
  </p>

  <h2 id="doors">1 · Two doors, both yours</h2>
  <p>
    While the desk is open, a Streamable-HTTP door answers on your own machine —
    nothing to install, nothing to spawn:
  </p>
  <pre><code>http://127.0.0.1:5181/mcp</code></pre>
  <p>
    And for clients that prefer to launch their own servers, the same desk
    speaks stdio through the installed binary:
  </p>
  <pre><code>said-and-done mcp</code></pre>
  <p>
    Both doors serve the same twenty-four tools and the same board. The HTTP
    door lives and dies with the window — the desk closed means no agent gets
    in. Settings → Connections carries the MCP switch and a copy button with
    your install's exact line; Doctor probes the door live and counts the tools.
  </p>

  <h2 id="connect">2 · Claude Code, in one line</h2>
  <p>
    Register the door once, then just talk. Here is the whole thing, recorded
    live — the setup line, then a question about the sample project's{" "}
    <em>Fuel &amp; Flight</em> board:
  </p>
  <SaidShot
    src="/said-and-done/shots/mcp/mcp-connect.gif"
    alt="A real terminal session: claude mcp add registers the said-and-done HTTP door, then Claude reads the Fuel & Flight board and lists every lane and card"
    caption="One line to connect, one question — Claude reads the whole board, lane by lane."
  />
  <p>
    Zed, Cursor and Windsurf take the same two facts — the HTTP URL above, or
    the stdio command — pasted into their own MCP server settings. Whichever
    client you run, the server introduces itself as{" "}
    <strong>Said and Done.</strong> and hands over the desk's tools.
  </p>

  <h2 id="real-work">3 · Ask for something real</h2>
  <p>
    Reading is a parlor trick. The desk earns its keep when the agent files work
    — here Claude is asked to create a card on Fuel &amp; Flight, land it
    straight in todo, and leave a comment explaining the urgency. Watch the id
    come back:
  </p>
  <SaidShot
    src="/said-and-done/shots/mcp/mcp-file.gif"
    alt="A real terminal session: Claude files MARS-53, Dust filter swap, straight into the todo lane with a comment, and reports the id back"
    caption="MARS-53 is born — filed to todo, commented, and the desk even suggested a note reference on its own."
  />
  <p>
    Note the last line: the desk auto-suggested a reference to
    <code>breathing-easy.md</code> from the project's own notes — new cards get their
    references scanned at birth, whoever files them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="board">4 · The card is on the board</h2>
  <p>
    Switch to the desk and there it is — <strong>MARS-53</strong> sitting in todo
    with its comment count and its reference, next to the cards you made yourself.
    No import, no sync, no refresh button. It was always the same board.
  </p>
  <SaidShot
    src="/said-and-done/shots/mcp/mcp-board.png"
    alt="The Said and Done. desktop board — MARS-53, Dust filter swap, filed by Claude, sitting in the todo lane with a comment and a reference"
    caption="Claude's card in the todo lane — one comment, one reference, signed work."
  />

  <h2 id="signed">5 · Signed, every time</h2>
  <p>
    Open MARS-53 as a case file and the history reads like a ledger:
    <code>agent:claude-code</code> created it, moved it, commented on it. An agent's
    name comes from its own MCP handshake, so the same assistant keeps one name across
    a whole session. When a card moved and you didn't move it, the case file says
    exactly who did — there is no anonymous move in this house.
  </p>
  <SaidShot
    src="/said-and-done/shots/casefile.png"
    alt="A case file where agent:quartermaster reports in the thread, signed by name"
    caption="The thread keeps everyone honest — agents report under their own names, next to yours."
  />

  <h2 id="together">6 · Work the same card together</h2>
  <p>
    Nobody owns a card. You can be editing while an agent leaves a note; its
    words land in the thread under its name, next to yours. From your phone, the
    remote client shows the same desk the agents are working — file a card from
    the couch, let the agent pick it up at the keyboard.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">What it can't do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>It can't edit your markdown — your files aren't its to touch.</li>
    <li>It can't act unasked — an agent works when you set it working.</li>
    <li>
      It can't reach in from outside — the doors answer your machine only.
    </li>
    <li>It can't hide — every move is signed and kept.</li>
  </ul>
</SaidDocsLayout>
